[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 2"""
 date="2026-10-05T14:24:11Z"
 content="""
Right, the reason I think the mirror would need to proxy the drop to the
original repository is that otherwise a client who dropped a file from the
mirror would update the location log to say it's no longer in the original
repository when it still is.

But the mirror doesn't need to be able to write to the original repository.
It would be ok for a drop from it to fail if it is unable to proxy the drop
on to the original repository. It could choose to always make drops
from it fail. Or it could choose to drop its local cache on a drop, and
then fail the drop, leaving the content in the original repository. Any of
these options would leave the git-annex branch in a consistent state.

(I didn't cover what the mirror would do if a client tried to store an
object on it, but similar reasoning applies.)

> In my sketch of an approach, I saw the mirror repo as an ordinary node in the git-annex network. It would have its own UUID, and therefore would require (ordinary) coordinated merges of the git-annex branch.

That's fine as long as you don't mind having the git-annex ref not be
identical to the one in the original repository.
"""]]
